  "description": "Design an ATM: a State machine for the session, a backtracking Chain of Responsibility for notes, an Adapter to a legacy bank core, and idempotent debit and reversal with tombstones, in Java, Python and C++14.",
  "hero": {
    "tag": "header",
    "attrs": {
      "class": "page-hero"
    },
    "html": "\n  <div class=\"hero-meta\">\n    <span class=\"level-badge\" data-level=\"3\">Level 3 - Intermediate</span>\n    <span class=\"time\">Read 70 min</span><span class=\"chip\">Build 120 min</span>\n    <span class=\"chip\">Machine-coding case study</span>\n    <span class=\"chip\">Rank 25 of 45</span>\n  </div>\n  <h1>ATM</h1>\n  <p class=\"lead\">A card goes in, a PIN is typed, notes come out, and the account is debited exactly once, even when the bank says no, the cassettes run short, or the note feeder jams halfway. This page builds the classic answer: a State machine for the session, a Chain of Responsibility for the notes, and an Adapter for the bank, in Java, Python and C++14, with a demo that proves each rule.</p>\n"
  },
